# Mongoose
An ODM(Object Document Mapper) library for MongoDB and Node.js.
It provides a structured way to work with MongoDB from JavaScript.

Without Mongoose, MongoDB allows flexible document structures:

Student 1
{
    name: "Kamal",
    age: 23
}

Student 2
{
    name: "Ram",
    course: "Computer Engineering",
    semester: 7
}
But application level system needs consistency.
Mongoose allows us to define rules for how documents should look. For Example:

Student
├── name       → String, required
├── email      → String, required, unique
├── age        → Number, min: 16
└── course     → String, required

so mongoose provides Schema defination, Data validation, Models, CRUD helpers, Middlewares/hooks, Query building, Type casting, Better organization of mongodb code.

* Mongoose doesnot replace MongoDB MongoDB is still responsible for storing data. Mongoose provide an application-level abstraction over MongoDB.

# MongoDB vs Mongoose

| MongoDB                   | Mongoose                         |
| ------------------------- | -------------------------------- |
| Database                  | ODM library                      |
| Stores documents          | Provides structure for documents |
| Uses collections          | Uses schemas and models          |
| Native MongoDB operations | JavaScript-friendly methods      |
| Flexible schema           | Schema can enforce rules         |
| Database technology       | Node.js library                  |


# Native MongoDB Driver vs Mongoose

| Native MongoDB Driver          | Mongoose                    |
| ------------------------------ | --------------------------- |
| Direct MongoDB interaction     | Higher-level abstraction    |
| Flexible structure             | Schema-based structure      |
| Manual validation often needed | Built-in schema validation  |
| Collections/documents          | Models/documents            |
| Native MongoDB queries         | Mongoose query API          |
| Less abstraction               | More abstraction            |
| More control                   | More developer conveniences |
| No ODM layer                   | ODM layer                   |


# What is ODM
Object Document Mapper
MongoDB stores data as documents. JS works with objects. Mongoose help to map JS object to MongoDB document.

# Installing Mongoose 
`npm install mongoose`


# What is Schema
A schema defines the structure and rules of documents handled by Mongoose.

const studentSchema = new mongoose.Schema({
    name: String,
    age: Number,
    course: String
});

* A schema does not directly represent the MongoDB collection. 
A model is created from the schema.

# Schema vs Model vs Document
Schema : What should a document look like?
Model : How do I interact with documents in MongoDB?
Document : An actual piece of data stored in MongoDB.

# Tyes of mongoose
String
Number
Boolean
Date
Array
Object
ObjectId

# required : 
makes a field mandadory, without it validation fails.
name: {
    type: String,
    required: true
}

# min and max :
Primarly used with numbers.
age: {
    type: Number,
    min: 18,
    max: 60
}
age : 23 is valid and age : 16 is invalid.

# minlength and maxlength
Used with string
name: {
    type: String,
    minlength: 3,
    maxlength: 50
}

# enum
Restricts a field to a specific value 
role: {
    type: String,
    enum: ["student", "staff", "admin"]
}
valid: Student 
invalid : manager

# Deafult Values
We can provide a deafult values
active: {
    type: Boolean,
    default: true
}

# Unique
This is commonly used when an data should not be duplicated.
email: {
    type: String,
    required: true,
    unique: true
}

`unique: true` is not a normal Mongoose validator. 
It is used to create a MongoDB unique index, so duplicate-key errors should still be handled at the application level.

# Custom Validators
Mongoose also allows custom validation logic.
semester: {
    type: Number,
    validate: {
        validator: function(value) {
            return value >= 1 && value <= 8;
        },
        message: "Semester must be between 1 and 8"
    }
}
This allows us to implement application-specific rules.